notes:
Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — three P0 correctness findings (missing account_exists check on /set handlers, objects_mut silently registering accounts, change-log dedup drops recycled ids). All three are workspace-wide-shaped: the account_exists gap is in 3 other siblings, the objects_mut and dedup-loop shapes are in 6 siblings. None are security-grade for a single-user dev backend but all three break the JMAP wire contract for multi-account deployments. Test coverage gap (no unknown-account /set tests) is the upstream cause; cookie-cutter propagation took 3 siblings down with it. demo_next_id divergence from canonical mail-server is the meta-finding: the canonical-template rule was reversed by commit 920fb0d. The crate is solid in handler-layer JMAP spec enforcement (RFC 9670 §3.3 forbidden + destroy-array validation are defended by tests).
Confidence: 15-20% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: JMAP-3t94.3 (recycled-id dedup) — confirming the trigger requires the demo_next_id collision (JMAP-qz9v.14) which only fires on default feature; under realistic-demo-ids the collision is essentially impossible, so .3's trigger is gated by .5 not being addressed. Uncertainty driven by: read-only review with no probe of the dispatcher's behavior under unknown accountId for /set specifically — I traced the code path but did not execute a /set call against an unknown accountId and observe the actual response. Would raise confidence if: a probe test in test_support that dispatches Principal/set with accountId='nonexistent' and asserts on the wire response shape (top-level type vs notCreated{c1{type}}), and a probe that constructs a recycled-id sequence (create-destroy-create with the deterministic feature) and runs /changes against it. Both probes were declined per the read-only rule; both should be reviewed-and-converted-into-tests by /do-beads JMAP-3t94 follow-up.
API contract risk: MEDIUM — one P1 (create_object id-on-O invariant unenforceable, already violated by in-tree MockBackend), four P2-P3 (MemoryError pub field, new_with_accounts canonical divergence, ClosureHandler version-pin, destroy_object underspec), six P3-P4 (URI re-export divergence, create_id semantics, supports_type contradiction, Clone semantics, stability statement scope, register_sharing_handlers re-registration semantics). No P0. The crate's wire-protocol behavior is correct (those findings live in JMAP-3t94.1-.12); these supplements are the layer above — contract clarity, version-pin discipline, and cookie-cutter alignment with the canonical mail-server. The strongest finding is .17 (create_object id invariant) because it has both a known in-tree violator (MockBackend admits it in a code comment) and silent wire breakage as the failure mode. The cookie-cutter divergences (.14 new_with_accounts, .16 URI re-export, .21 MemoryError doc) all point at the same underlying cause: this crate was written without the canonical-template propagation rule being enforced. A workspace-wide alignment pass would resolve those three plus reveal similar divergences elsewhere.
API contract confidence: 25-30% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: .22 (MemoryBackend Clone semantics doc gap — real concern but narrow; arguably workspace-wide rather than sharing-server-specific), .25 (backend.rs bulk re-export module-doc — mild maintainability finding, identical pattern in canonical mail-server). Strongest finding: .17 (create_object id-on-O invariant unenforceable; MockBackend in the same crate openly admits violating it). Uncertainty driven by: (1) did not cross-validate every re-exported jmap-server type against upstream stability promises, (2) did not run cargo public-api or similar surface-diff tool against the 0.1.0 → 0.1.1 baseline (no changelog exists for this crate), (3) did not trace into jmap-sharing-types to verify PrincipalProperty / ShareNotificationProperty are #[non_exhaustive], (4) did not exhaustively walk Cargo feature combinations — memory + realistic-demo-ids may expose surface I missed. Would raise confidence if: a cargo public-api diff run against the baseline, plus a trace into jmap-sharing-types for the two property-selector enums.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — competent idiomatic Rust with minor surface inconsistencies (HashMap key allocation on lookup, to_string().as_str() redundancy, &mut self on Arc<Mutex<>> mock methods, missing Default on test mock); no soundness issues, no unsafe, no Box<dyn Error> in library API, clippy is clean.
Rust idiom confidence: 20-25% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: .28 (&mut self on add_notification — real but might be intentional convention for test fixtures to make multi-task fixture-setup forbidden), .31 (now labeled opinion — picking one of two valid serde-infallibility idioms is taste-grade). Strongest finding: .26 (HashMap tuple-key allocates String on every read-side lookup; affects 6 sibling MemoryBackends, real per-call cost, idiomatic restructure is mechanical). Uncertainty driven by: (1) didn't run cargo expand to see what the reg! macro actually generates — it could have additional non-idiomatic shapes I missed; (2) didn't verify that the AccountState fields (principals/notifications) have idiomatic accessor patterns vs. direct field manipulation; (3) didn't check whether the trait's impl Future<...> + Send syntax follows the workspace's settled pattern across all 7 server crates or if this crate diverges; (4) read-only review per /review-rusty rules — couldn't probe macro expansion or measure allocation cost. Would raise confidence if: cargo expand on register_sharing_handlers to inspect the generated closure-and-Arc shape, plus a heap-allocation flamegraph of the demo MemoryBackend under a typical /set load.
API contract risk (supplement): LOW — the prior /review-library pass already filed comprehensive contract findings (.13-.25); this supplement adds 2 P2 underspecified findings on create_object / update_object error+state contracts (parallel to the closed .19 destroy_object work) and 2 P3 findings (.34 versioning over-promise on transition default-method bodies, .35 trait-doc gap on caller-identity foundation seam). No new immutability, leakage, or defensive findings — those categories were saturated by the prior pass.
API contract supplement confidence: 15-20% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: .34 (transition default-method bodies — workspace-architectural concern that arguably belongs on the canonical mail-server, not this sibling), .35 (caller-identity trait-doc gap — same cookie-cutter shape). Strongest findings: .32 (update_object) and .33 (create_object), both direct parallels to the closed .19 destroy_object gap. Uncertainty driven by: (1) did not exhaustively diff this crate's public surface against canonical jmap-mail-server to find additional cookie-cutter divergences in unexamined surfaces (e.g. method-level rustdoc on handle_*); (2) did not check whether jmap-sharing-types' Principal / ShareNotification types have their own contract gaps that propagate through the re-exports here; (3) the prior /review-library pass already saturated the contract surface — 13 contract findings already filed — so this supplement is mostly fill-in-gaps work on the trait method docs.
Idiom rating (second-pass /review-rusty after JMAP-3t94 cleanup commits 5d516e1 and 8ecc0eb): CLEAN — no soundness issues, no unsafe, clippy is clean on default settings, three new findings filed (.36 P3 manual_let_else, .37 P3 MockBackend val-mutation pattern divergence within crate, .38 P4 redundant inner use [opinion-labeled]). The cleanup commits did not introduce regressions; both findings .36 and .37 are pre-existing shapes that the first /review-rusty pass missed. The cookie-cutter intent of the crate is largely intact and the engineering-basis findings are mechanical fixes.
Rust idiom confidence (second-pass): 10-15% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: .37 (MockBackend val-mutation pattern — defensive if-let-vs-direct-IndexMut is a known panic-vs-silent-skip tradeoff; whether to flag the silent-skip side as a bug or a feature depends on how strictly the .17 invariant is enforced at the trait level, which is still being settled in the open .33 contract bead). Strongest finding: .36 (manual_let_else — clippy fires loudly on -W pedantic, fix is mechanical, and the same shape exists in 4 sibling MemoryBackends so the workspace sweep is high-leverage). Uncertainty driven by: (1) did not exhaustively diff sharing-server against canonical mail-server line-by-line for sub-paragraph idiom drift; (2) did not run -W clippy::nursery on top of -W clippy::pedantic (nursery lints can surface unconventional patterns that pedantic alone misses, but at the cost of higher false-positive rates); (3) the cleanup commits 5d516e1 + 8ecc0eb added ~778 net new lines, and although I read every line, fresh idiom drift can lurk in test_support and the new regression tests I scanned but didn't deeply analyze. Convergence indicators: ACCEPTABLE → CLEAN over two passes, only P3-P4 findings remain on idiom-rust label, one is opinion-labeled. The convergence rule per workspace AGENTS.md is satisfied — further /review-rusty passes on this scope would be bikeshedding.
